#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:234
msgid "Security"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:240
msgid "Complex password rules are not user friendly; but security is mainly never :)"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:242
msgid "Of course, all passwords should be as secure as possible, but this is especially true for all accounts that have privileges (staff, admin, super-admin); you may explain your users why this is important."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:244
msgid "You can enforce some rules for members (and super-admin) passwords:"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:246
msgid "minimum length (6 characters or more),"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:247
msgid "minimum \"strength\","
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:248
msgid "blacklist,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:249
msgid "no personal information."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:251
msgid "Galette settings, security tab"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:256
msgid "Length is still the only rule that is active per default, just configure the number of characters required. On passwords fields, failures will be displayed on the fly; as well as a \"strength meter\" displayed for information."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:260
msgid "If you enable password checks, it is not possible to know if some of existing ones does not respect them. Galette will display a warning at login if checks are not respected, but login will still be possible!"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:262
msgid "But wait... Password security is important, but Galette does not enforce nothing! Isn't that dumb? Well, not really. For tests or entirely private instances, security may be less important; and in some cases, being too restrictive may be an issue for your users; that's why this is up to you to secure as needed; just like using SSL or not :)"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:265
msgid "Password strength"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:267
msgid "Password strength calculation is quite simple. It is based on 4 rules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:269
msgid "contains lower case characters,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:270
msgid "contains upper case characters,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:271
msgid "contains number,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:272
msgid "contains special characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:274
msgid "You can choose between 5 values for strength configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:276
msgid "**none**: (default): disables strength checks and check for personal information,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:277
msgid "**weaker**: enables check for personal information, only one of the rule is mandatory,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:278
msgid "**medium**: two rules are mandatory,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:279
msgid "**strong**: three rules are mandatory,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:280
msgid "**very strong**: the four rules are mandatory."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:283
msgid "Blacklisted passwords"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:285
msgid "A default list of 500 common passwords is provided as a blacklist you can enable, \"galette\" is also blacklisted."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:289
msgid "The ``galette/data/blacklist.txt`` file is used to list blacklisted terms (one per line). You can provide your own file, we advice you to complete the existing one."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:292
msgid "Personal information as password"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:294
msgid "This check rely on strength activation (all but **none** level). For the super-admin account, this will just ensure you are not using login as password. For standard accounts, there are several information involved:"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:296
msgid "name,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:297
msgid "surname,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:298
msgid "nickname,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:299
msgid "login,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:300
msgid "email,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:301
msgid "birthdate,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:302
msgid "town"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:304
msgid "Basically, user cannot use verbatim any of those information as password. Some possible combinations are also checked, like surname and name couple (or name and surname), first letter of surname with name, etc. Birthdate will be checked in different formats as well (localized, international, and some variants)."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:309
msgid "Two-factor authentication"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:313
msgid "Marked **experimental**: it is complete and tested, but it is new, and the interface may still change. It is disabled by default; nothing happens until you choose otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:315
msgid "A password is a single factor: whoever knows it is in. Two-factor authentication asks, right after the password, for a six digits code that changes every thirty seconds and comes from an application on the user own phone or computer. A stolen password is then not enough anymore."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:317
msgid "Galette implements the TOTP standard (:rfc:`6238`), the one every authenticator application speaks; no third party service is involved, and nothing leaves your server."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:319
msgid "Two policies are available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:321
msgid "**disabled** (default): nothing changes, nobody is asked for a code,"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:322
msgid "**optional**: everybody may enable it from their own account, nobody has to."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:324
msgid "Making the second factor **compulsory** — for administrators and staff members, or for everyone — is written and tested, but it is not offered yet: under a compulsory policy, a server clock that drifts or an enrolment that goes wrong puts a whole association outside its own instance, and the only way back is a command in the database. It is planned for a later version, once the optional policy has been used in the field."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:326
msgid "Since nobody is forced to enrol, administrators and staff members — the accounts that can read the civil status, the addresses and the financial data of every member — are invited to set a second factor up when they log in, with a **Later** and a **Do not ask again** button. Declining is remembered in a cookie of the browser, so the invitation comes back on a new browser, or after a year."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:330
msgid "Switching the policy back to **disabled** does not delete anything. Codes are no longer asked for, and the second factors already enabled start being asked for again as soon as you enable a policy back. While the policy is disabled, the pages to enable a second factor answer nothing: a factor enrolled then would never be asked for."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:332
msgid "The super administrator is covered as well, as any other account. As it is not a member, it has no recovery codes; see :ref:`what to do should you lose it <faq_2fa>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:334
msgid "Members enable and manage their own second factor from their account; this is described in :ref:`the members part of this manual <man_2fa>`. Administrators and staff members can :ref:`reset the second factor of a member <member_2fa_reset>` who lost it."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:337
msgid "Super administrator credentials"
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:341
msgid "The super administrator login and password are no longer in the preferences. Once logged in as super administrator, go to **My account**, then **My information**. Galette asks for the current password before saving a new one."
msgstr ""

#: ../usermanual/preferences.rst:343
msgid "Should that password be lost, it can be changed :ref:`from the command line <cli_superadmin_password>`."
msgstr ""
